<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <!-- Fields the shop no longer has any use for.

         369 Mart sells electrical and electronic goods. The catalogue was
         started from a grocery template, so a Wi-Fi adapter's form asked
         whether it was vegetarian, how long it kept, and what its net
         quantity was. All three are gone from the generator table and so
         from fields.xml.

         Dropping a record from a data file is normally enough - Odoo removes
         what a module no longer declares. Not here: fields.xml is
         noupdate="1", so a shopkeeper's switches survive an upgrade, and the
         same flag makes Odoo skip the record over the obsolete-data sweep.
         The three rows stayed in the database, still on the Product Page
         screen, still hiding boxes on the product form. Hence saying it
         outright.

         Deleting the field takes its category values and its per-product
         overrides with it - both are ondelete='cascade'.

         Not noupdate, so it runs on every upgrade and a row that somehow
         returns is removed again. -->
    <delete model="mart369.product.field"
            search="[('key', 'in', ['veg', 'net_quantity', 'shelf_life'])]"/>
</odoo>
